for advanced packaging equipment makers. Take the accelerator rack and take it apart at the coolant

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:59.440
because the limit is heat leaving the cabinet. Data Center Knowledge, citing Bill Claimon,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:06.860
says AI work is pushing a typical rack from roughly 10 to 30 kilowatts into 80 to 150 kilowatts

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.000
with advanced racks approaching one megawatt. Air cooling is a volume problem.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:18.220
Fans drive room air over a heat sink and a coil somewhere else in the building takes the heat back out.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.680
More flow means more pressure drop and a hotter chance that aisle air returns to the inlet.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.640
The electrical feed and the airflow fail at the same moment.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.120
Another card then adds temperature, not useful work.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.600
Liquid does not erase the heat, it changes the carrier.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.680
A pump moves water or a dielectric over a cold plate, or the board sits in a bath, and

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.800
a coolant distribution unit hands the load to the building loop.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.060
A small liquid flow carries what would have needed a large rush of air.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:53.380
Clemen tied that shift to AFCOM State of the Data Center 2026 survey.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.740
69% of respondents expect rack density to jump like nothing they have seen, and liquid

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.480
